### Step 3: Detailed Breakdown of Every Field

Here is what each section of the manifest does under the hood:

| Field | Value in Lab | Production Explanation |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `apiVersion` | `argoproj.io/v1alpha1` | The ArgoCD API group for Application resources. |
| `kind` | `Application` | The custom resource that ArgoCD's controller watches. |
| `metadata.name` | `guestbook` | Unique application identifier displayed in the CLI and UI. |
| `metadata.namespace` | `argocd` | **CRITICAL:** Must be `argocd`. The application controller only monitors its own control-plane namespace. |
| `metadata.finalizers` | `resources-finalizer.argocd.argoproj.io` | Official cascading deletion hook. When you delete this Application, ArgoCD safely deletes all child pods, services, and deployments instead of leaving orphaned resources. |
| `spec.project` | `default` | Security boundary. Controls permitted Git repos, destination clusters, and resource whitelists/blacklists. |
| `spec.source.repoURL` | `https://github.com/argoproj/argocd-example-apps.git` | Git repository containing raw Kubernetes YAMLs, Helm charts, or Kustomize overlays. |
| `spec.source.targetRevision`| `HEAD` | Git branch (`main`), tag (`v1.4.0`), or commit SHA to track. |
| `spec.source.path` | `guestbook` | Subdirectory inside the repository containing the manifests. |
| `spec.destination.server` | `https://kubernetes.default.svc` | In-cluster Kubernetes API server endpoint where ArgoCD is running. |
| `spec.destination.namespace`| `guestbook` | The target Kubernetes namespace where the application workloads will be deployed. |
| `spec.syncPolicy.syncOptions`| `CreateNamespace=true` | Built-in safeguard: instructs ArgoCD to automatically create the destination namespace if it does not already exist. |

> 💡 **Notice what is NOT here:** There is no `spec.syncPolicy.automated` block. In Day 11, we intentionally keep sync manual so we can inspect every transition of the reconciliation lifecycle. We automate this in Day 12!

